/**
@mainpage CTR Kinematics Library

A C++23 library implementing the forward and inverse kinematics of a
three-tube concentric tube robot (CTR), based on the Cosserat-theory
kinematic model of Rucker, Jones & Webster (*IEEE Transactions on Robotics*,
2010).

@section main_quickstart Quickstart

@code{.cpp}
#include <ctr/CTR.hpp>

using namespace ctr;

// Three tubes, innermost first. All quantities SI (m, Pa, 1/m).
std::array<Tube, NUM_TUBES> tubes = {
    Tube{{.OD = 0.92e-3, .ID = 0.80e-3, .E = 65e9, .G = 24.6e9,
          .ls = 190e-3, .lc = 60e-3, .u_ast = {25.0, 0.0, 0.0}}},
    Tube{{.OD = 1.10e-3, .ID = 0.97e-3, .E = 65e9, .G = 24.6e9,
          .ls = 120e-3, .lc = 80e-3, .u_ast = {10.0, 0.0, 0.0}}},
    Tube{{.OD = 1.40e-3, .ID = 1.20e-3, .E = 65e9, .G = 24.6e9,
          .ls = 90e-3,  .lc = 40e-3, .u_ast = {7.14, 0.0, 0.0}}},
};

// q = [β₁ β₂ β₃ | α₁ α₂ α₃]: retractions [m] (non-positive), rotations [rad].
const blaze::StaticVector<double, 6UL> q = {-120e-3, -100e-3, -80e-3, 0, 0, 0};

CTR robot(std::move(tubes), q, 1e-6);

bvp_type guess{};                              // zero = valid cold start
FKResult fk = robot.actuate(q, guess);         // forward kinematics
if (fk)
    auto tip = robot.tipPosition();            // [m]

IKResult ik = robot.solveIK(tip + blaze::StaticVector<double,3UL>{0, 5e-3, 0},
                            5e-4, guess);      // inverse kinematics
if (ik)
    auto qSolution = ik.q;
@endcode

@section main_pages Documentation pages

- @ref conventions — units, frames, joint vector layout, state layout
- @ref model — the Cosserat model, the shooting BVP, and the IK algorithm
- @ref building — dependencies, building, testing, consuming the package

@section main_design Design highlights

- **Honest results.** ctr::FKResult and ctr::IKResult report what actually
  happened; ctr::IKResult::converged is true only when the tip actually
  reached the target within tolerance.
- **Deterministic integration.** The backbone ODE is integrated with a
  fixed-step RK4 (default 1 mm; tip accuracy ≈ 3×10⁻¹¹ m for tabletop-scale
  robots). Determinism is a feature: the finite-difference Jacobians rely on
  truncation-error cancellation between nominal and perturbed shots.
- **Exact task Jacobian.** CTR::kinematicJacobian differentiates on the BVP
  equilibrium manifold via the implicit function theorem — not at frozen
  shooting variables.
- **No hidden global state, no LAPACK/BLAS dependency.** Only Boost.Odeint
  (header-only) and Blaze.

@section main_cite Reference

D. C. Rucker, B. A. Jones and R. J. Webster III, "A Geometrically Exact
Model for Externally Loaded Concentric-Tube Continuum Robots,"
IEEE Transactions on Robotics, vol. 26, no. 5, pp. 769-780, 2010.
*/
